WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:05.669
The Mighty Thor is virtually synonymous with
his thundering warhammer, but unfortunately

00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:09.750
for him, Mjolnir didn’t make it through
Thor: Ragnarok intact.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:10.750
The good news?

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:16.640
In Avengers: Infinity War, Thor gets his godly
hands on an even more powerful weapon: Stormbreaker.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.220
If you’re curious about its mythological
roots and its cosmic comic book origins, here's

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.300
the truth behind Thor's new hammer.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.150
The Norse arsenal

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:30.769
Norse mythology is full of magical weaponry,
so it's probably not surprising that Stormbreaker

00:00:30.769 --> 00:00:34.000
isn't the only other tool wielded by the God
of Thunder.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.750
In fact, Thor rolled into battle strapped
with more magical weapons than anyone — including

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:40.210
Tyr, the god of war.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:44.320
Mjolnir is the most important, but if you're
only familiar with it from the comics and

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.140
movies, it might surprise you that the whole
thing about being worthy was never actually

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:49.840
part of the original myths.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.969
Instead, Thor was the only one who could heft
the hammer simply because it was really, really

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:58.160
heavy, to the point where even he needed special
equipment to lift it.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.980
The Megingjörð was a magic belt that doubled
Thor's already considerable strength, and

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:07.870
the Járngreipr, or "iron grippers," were
gauntlets that allowed him to control Mjolnir

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:10.370
despite its heavy weight and unbalanced handle.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:14.410
It's worth noting that the Megingjörð actually
does exist in the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:18.500
When Happy Hogan is clearing out Avengers
Tower in Spider-Man: Homecoming, he mentions

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.900
it as one of the items that's boxed up for
transport.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:24.000
“The meging…

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.040
Thor’s magic belt.”'

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:27.960
Beta Ray Bill

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.670
Unlike Mjolnir, Stormbreaker doesn't have
its origins in classic mythology.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:36.720
Instead, it's pure Marvel Comics, where it
belongs to another character: Beta Ray Bill.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.680
When legendary creator Walt Simonson arrived
on the book in 1983, the first thing he did

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.030
was tell a story about what would happen if
someone else was worthy enough to heft Thor's

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:47.030
hammer.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.820
That someone was a super-powered alien.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:53.560
Beta Ray Bill was chosen to defend his people
as they traveled through space as refugees,

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.420
and fought Thor to a stalemate due to a misunderstanding.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:00.370
During the fight, his noble heart allowed
him to lift the hammer and take the power

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:01.370
himself.

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:05.100
Once Odin found out that someone else had
taken his son's power, his response was to

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:10.259
demand that Thor and Bill fight each other
to the death in the fiery realm of Skartheim.

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:14.850
Not exactly the best example of parenting,
especially since Bill won that one, too.

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:18.060
Instead of fighting to the death, though,
Bill rescued Thor and brought him back to

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.090
Asgard, a test of his worthiness that was
Odin’s plan all along.

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:26.860
To reward Bill, Odin convinced Eitri and the
dwarves of Nidavellir to craft a second hammer:

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:27.860
Stormbreaker.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:32.379
It gave Bill all of Thor's powers, and with
that, Thor and Bill became "oath brothers”

00:02:32.379 --> 00:02:33.390
and allies.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:36.760
While he hasn't really appeared in person,
we do known that Bill, or at least someone

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:40.310
who looks an awful lot like him, does exist
in the MCU.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:44.680
In Thor: Ragnarok, you can briefly spot a
sculpture of Bill's head at the Grandmaster's

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.590
gladiatorial arena, along with a villain called
the Bi-Beast.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:49.680
No, really.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.350
The Mace, the Storm, and the Frogly

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:56.830
Beyond Stormbreaker, there are a couple of
other Asgardian weapons of note in the Marvel

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:57.830
Universe.

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:02.180
The most prominent is probably Thunderstrike's
mace, Thunderstrike, and yes: the dude and

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.960
the weapon both have the same name.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.530
It turns out Eric Masterson is not an exceptionally
creative superhero.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:11.621
He was bonded with Thor for two years in the
‘90s, and when they were separated, Odin

00:03:11.621 --> 00:03:14.930
gifted him a magical mace he could use to
continue fighting evil.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:19.280
It’s functionally identical to Mjolnir,
but with one major difference: its inscription

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.360
reads "The World Still Needs Heroes."

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.790
Drop that one on your opponents next time
you get in some Marvel Comics trivia.

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:29.410
There’s also Stormcaster, a hammer that
Loki once gave to Storm of the X-Men in yet

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:31.930
another attempt to cause trouble for Thor.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:35.709
Considering that Storm can already call down
lightning to obliterate whatever she chooses,

00:03:35.709 --> 00:03:40.050
and that she's already been worshipped as
a goddess on Earth, this seems a little excessive.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:41.600
That might be why she didn’t keep it.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.240
The Marvel Universe's weirdest hammer, however,
comes from a story where Loki turned Thor

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:47.490
into a frog.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:51.710
When he recovered his hammer, Thor struck
it on the ground and transformed into… a

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:56.750
six-foot, six-inch frog with a helmet and
a magic hammer, because comics are great.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:00.930
Several years later, another frog who had
once been human was revealed to have hefted

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:05.740
a sliver of uru that broke off of Mjolnir
in that story, transforming himself into the

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.300
tiny Frog of Thunder, Throg.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.630
Jarnbjorn, the Iron Bear

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:15.110
Thor wasn't always worthy enough to lift Mjolnir,
and during his centuries as a hot-headed,

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:18.790
arrogant brawler, he had another weapon of
choice when it was time to go bust up some

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:21.820
frost giants: an axe named Jarnbjorn.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:26.000
Norwegian for “Iron Bear,” the name is
fitting - it's a weapon that's almost as tough

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.160
as Mjolnir.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:28.450
And it's a good thing, too.

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:32.761
When Thor doubted his own worthiness and found
himself unable to lift it, Mjolnir found a

00:04:32.761 --> 00:04:35.760
new wielder in the form of a mysterious female
Thor.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.030
The Odinson took up Jarnbjorn once again,
because to a man without a hammer, every problem

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.960
looks like you could probably solve it with
an axe.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.750
Ultimate Mjolnir

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:49.220
In terms of visuals, the MCU's version of
Stormbreaker doesn't quite resemble Beta Ray

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:51.720
Bill's hammer, although it has the same basic
shape.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.320
And they call it an axe but whatever… same
difference.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.660
Anyway, it bears a striking resemblance to
the version of Mjolnir carried by the Thor

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.440
of the Ultimate Universe.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:04.590
If you're not familiar with it, Ultimate Marvel
was a publishing imprint that kicked off in

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:08.500
2000 with a more cinematic style, but the
characters didn't take long to diverge pretty

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:11.320
wildly from their core-universe counterparts.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:15.150
With Thor, readers were initially meant to
be unsure of whether he was a mythical god

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:19.000
brought to Earth or a delusional but extremely
handsome mental patient.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:23.389
Either way, his suit and hammer were technological,
not magical… at first.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:27.370
Eventually, his godhood was confirmed, and
it was revealed that the techno-Mjolnir he'd

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:31.010
carried was designed to look like his memories
of his actual godly hammer.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:35.710
The Ultimate Universe was eventually destroyed,
but the hammer found its way to the core Marvel

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:39.830
Universe, where it wound up in the hands of
the extremely unlikely Volstagg the Voluminous,

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:42.990
who was transformed into "War Thor."

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.840
The cinematic Stormbreaker

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:49.460
Thor's quest for a new hammer is a major plot
point in Infinity War, and it lifts a lot

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:50.470
from the comics.

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:55.150
In this version, Nidavellir is a forge in
space orbiting a dying star, and the hammer

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:58.540
forged by Eitri is basically an upgraded version
of Mjolnir.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:02.710
In addition to the usual flying and smashing,
it also gives Thor the ability to control

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:06.470
the Bifrost, which was previously a property
of only Heimdall's sword.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:09.240
There's one more really cool thing about the
MCU's Stormbreaker, though.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:14.190
In a representation of the disparate parts
of the MCU coming together for Infinity War,

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:18.430
the hammer is literally built from pieces
of the Avengers and the Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:22.590
While the heads were forged from uru in Nidavellir,
they were joined by a handle that was made

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:24.890
from Groot's arm, bringing everything together.

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:26.180
Thanks for watching!

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:28.620
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.420
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

